AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2011B", "label": "2011 B", "problems": [{"q": "<p> What is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{2+4+6}{1+3+5}-\\dfrac{1+3+5}{2+4+6} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{36}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{12}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{147}{60}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{43}{3}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">-1</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{36}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{12}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{147}{60}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{43}{3}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The given expression is equal to</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{12}{9}-\\dfrac{9}{12}=\\dfrac{4}{3}-\\dfrac{3}{4}=\\dfrac{16-9}{12}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{7}{12}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> Josanna's test scores to date are <span class=\"tex\">90,80,70,60</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">85</span>. Her goal is to raise her test average at least <span class=\"tex\">3</span> points with her next test. What is the minimum test score she would need to accomplish this goal?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">80</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">82</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">85</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">90</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">95</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">80</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">82</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">85</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">90</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">95</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The sum of her first 5 test scores is 385 , yielding an average of 77. To raise her average to 80 , her <span class=\"tex\">6^{\\text {th }}</span> test score must be the difference between <span class=\"tex\">6 \\cdot 80=480</span> and 385 , which is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{95}</span> .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> LeRoy and Bernardo went on a week-long trip together and agreed to share the costs equally. Over the week, each of them paid for various joint expenses such as gasoline and car rental. At the end of the trip it turned out that LeRoy had paid <span class=\"tex\">A</span> dollars and Bernardo had paid <span class=\"tex\">B</span> dollars, where <span class=\"tex\">A&lt;B</span>. How many dollars must LeRoy give to Bernardo so that they share the costs equally?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{A+B}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{A-B}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{B-A}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">B-A</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">A+B</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{A+B}{2}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{A-B}{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{B-A}{2}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">B-A</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">A+B</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Bernardo has paid <span class=\"tex\">B-A</span> dollars more than LeRoy. If LeRoy gives Bernardo half of that difference, <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\dfrac{B-A}{2}}</span>, then each will have paid the same amount.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> In multiplying two positive integers <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, Ron reversed the digits of the twodigit number <span class=\"tex\">a</span>. His erroneous product was <span class=\"tex\">161</span>. What is the correct value of the product of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">116</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">161</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">204</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">214</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">224</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">116</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">161</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">204</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">214</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">224</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">161=23 \\cdot 7</span>, the only two digit factor of 161 is 23 . The correct product must have been <span class=\"tex\">32 \\cdot 7= \\boxed{224}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">N</span> be the second smallest positive integer that is divisible by every positive integer less than <span class=\"tex\">7</span>. What is the sum of the digits of <span class=\"tex\">N?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">9</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">N</span> is divisible by 3,4 , and 5 , the prime factorization of <span class=\"tex\">N</span> must contain one 3 , two <span class=\"tex\">2 \\mathrm{~s}</span>, and one 5 . Furthermore <span class=\"tex\">2^{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5=60</span> is divisible by every integer less than 7. Therefore the numbers with this property are precisely the positive multiples of 60 . The second smallest positive multiple of 60 is 120 , and the sum of its digits is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{3}</span> .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> Two tangents to a circle are drawn from a point <span class=\"tex\">A</span>. The points of contact <span class=\"tex\">B</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C</span> divide the circle into arcs with lengths in the ratio <span class=\"tex\">2: 3</span>. What is the degree measure of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A C?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">24</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">36</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">48</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">60</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">24</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">30</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">36</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">48</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">60</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> be the center of the circle, and let the degree measures of the minor and major arcs be <span class=\"tex\">2 x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">3 x</span>, respectively. Because <span class=\"tex\">2 x+3 x=360^{\\circ}</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">x=72^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B O C=2 x=144^{\\circ}</span>. In quadrilateral <span class=\"tex\">A B O C</span>, the segments <span class=\"tex\">A B</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A C</span> are tangent to the circle, thus <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A B O=\\angle A C O=90^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A C=360^{\\circ}-\\left(144^{\\circ}+90^{\\circ}+90^{\\circ}\\right)= \\boxed{36^{\\circ}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> be two-digit positive integers with mean <span class=\"tex\">60</span>. What is the maximum value of the ratio <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{x}{y}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{33}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{39}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{99}{10}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{33}{7}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{39}{7}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">9</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{99}{10}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">x \\leq 99</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}(x+y)=60</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">y=120-x \\geq</span> <span class=\"tex\">120-99=21</span>. Thus the maximum value of <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{x}{y}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{99}{21}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{33}{7}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> Keiko walks once around a track at exactly the same constant speed every day. The sides of the track are straight, and the ends are semicircles. The track has width <span class=\"tex\">6</span> meters, and it takes her <span class=\"tex\">36</span> seconds longer to walk around the outside edge of the track than around the inside edge. What is Keiko's speed in meters per second?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2023_11_14_f0ca9305ab054e45278bg-125.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4 \\pi}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5 \\pi}{3}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\pi</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4 \\pi}{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5 \\pi}{3}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The only parts of the track that are longer walking on the outside edge rather than the inside edge are the two semicircular portions. If the radius of the inner semicircle is <span class=\"tex\">r</span>, then the difference in the lengths of the two paths is <span class=\"tex\">2 \\pi(r+6)-2 \\pi r=12 \\pi</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> be her speed in meters per second. Then <span class=\"tex\">36 x=12 \\pi</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">x= \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> Two real numbers are selected independently at random from the interval <span class=\"tex\">[-20,10]</span>. What is the probability that the product of those numbers is greater than zero?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{9}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{9}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{9}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Consider all ordered pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> with each of the numbers <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> in the closed interval <span class=\"tex\">[-20,10]</span>. These pairs fill a <span class=\"tex\">30 \\times 30</span> square in the coordinate plane, with an area of 900 square units. Ordered pairs in the first and third quadrants have the desired property, namely <span class=\"tex\">a \\cdot b&gt;0</span>. The areas of the portions of the <span class=\"tex\">30 \\times 30</span> square in the first and third quadrants are <span class=\"tex\">10^{2}=100</span> and <span class=\"tex\">20^{2}=400</span>, respectively. Therefore the probability of a positive product is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{100+400}{900}=\\dfrac{5}{9}</span>.</p>\n<p>\\section*{OR}<br/>\nEach of the numbers is positive with probability <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span> and negative with probability <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span>. Their product is positive if and only if both numbers are positive or both are negative, so the requested probability is <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)^{2}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{5}{9}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> Rectangle <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> has <span class=\"tex\">A B=6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B C=3</span>. Point <span class=\"tex\">M</span> is chosen on side <span class=\"tex\">A B</span> so that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A M D=\\angle C M D</span>. What is the degree measure of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A M D?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">45</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">60</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">75</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">15</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">30</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">45</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">60</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">75</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Sides <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> are parallel, so <span class=\"tex\">\\angle C D M=\\angle A M D</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A M D=\\angle C M D</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C M D</span> is isosceles and <span class=\"tex\">C D=C M=6</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle M C B</span> is a <span class=\"tex\">30-60-90^{\\circ}</span> right triangle with <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B M C=30^{\\circ}</span>. Finally, <span class=\"tex\">2 \\cdot \\angle A M D+30^{\\circ}=\\angle A M D+\\angle C M D+30^{\\circ}=180^{\\circ}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A M D= \\boxed{75^{\\circ}}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-215.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> A frog located at <span class=\"tex\">(x, y)</span>, with both <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> integers, makes successive jumps of length <span class=\"tex\">5</span> and always lands on points with integer coordinates. Suppose that the frog starts at <span class=\"tex\">(0,0)</span> and ends at <span class=\"tex\">(1,0)</span>. What is the smallest possible number of jumps the frog makes?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">3</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">5</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">6</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">A B=1</span>, the smallest number of jumps is at least 2 . The perpendicular bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> is the line with equation <span class=\"tex\">x=\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>, which has no points with integer coordinates, so 2 jumps are not possible. A sequence of <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{3}</span> jumps is possible; one such sequence is <span class=\"tex\">(0,0)</span> to <span class=\"tex\">(3,4)</span> to <span class=\"tex\">(6,0)</span> to <span class=\"tex\">(1,0)</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> A dart board is a regular octagon divided into regions as shown. Suppose that a dart thrown at the board is equally likely to land anywhere on the board. What is the probability that the dart lands within the center square?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2023_11_14_f0ca9305ab054e45278bg-126.jpg\" width=\"300\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2-\\sqrt{2}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">2-\\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Assume the octagon's edge is 1. Then the corner triangles have hypotenuse 1 and thus legs <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}</span> and area <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span> each; the four rectangles are 1 by <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}</span> and have area <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}</span> each, and the center square has area 1. The total area is <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4}+4 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1=2+2 \\sqrt{2}</span>. The probability that the dart hits the center square is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2+2 \\sqrt{2}}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{2}}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-216.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> Brian writes down four integers <span class=\"tex\">w&gt;x&gt;y&gt;z</span> whose sum is <span class=\"tex\">44</span>. The pairwise positive differences of these numbers are <span class=\"tex\">1,3,4,5,6</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">9</span>. What is the sum of the possible values for <span class=\"tex\">w?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">16</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">31</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">48</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">62</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">93</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">16</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">31</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">48</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">62</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">93</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>The largest pairwise difference is 9 , so <span class=\"tex\">w-z=9</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">n</span> be either <span class=\"tex\">x</span> or <span class=\"tex\">y</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">n</span> is between <span class=\"tex\">w</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">9=w-z=(w-n)+(n-z)</span></p><p>Therefore the positive differences <span class=\"tex\">w-n</span> and <span class=\"tex\">n-z</span> must sum to 9 . The given pairwise differences that sum to 9 are <span class=\"tex\">3+6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">4+5</span>. The remaining pairwise difference must be <span class=\"tex\">x-y=1</span>.</p>\n<p>The second largest pairwise difference is 6 , so either <span class=\"tex\">w-y=6</span> or <span class=\"tex\">x-z=6</span>. In the first case the set of four numbers may be expressed as <span class=\"tex\">\\{w, w-5, w-6, w-9\\}</span>. Hence <span class=\"tex\">4 w-20=44</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">w=16</span>. In the second case <span class=\"tex\">w-x=3</span>, and the four numbers may be expressed as <span class=\"tex\">\\{w, w-3, w-4, w-9\\}</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">4 w-16=44</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">w=15</span>. The sum of the possible values for <span class=\"tex\">w</span> is <span class=\"tex\">16+15= \\boxed{31}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: The possible sets of four numbers are <span class=\"tex\">\\{16,11,10,7\\}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\{15,12,11,6\\}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> A segment through the focus <span class=\"tex\">F</span> of a parabola with vertex <span class=\"tex\">V</span> is perpendicular to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{F V}</span> and intersects the parabola in points <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (\\angle A V B)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{4}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{3}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{7}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{4}{5}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{3}{5}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{1}{2}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">\\ell</span> be the directrix of the parabola, and let <span class=\"tex\">C</span> and <span class=\"tex\">D</span> be the projections of <span class=\"tex\">F</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span> onto <span class=\"tex\">\\ell</span>, respectively. For any point in the parabola, its distance to <span class=\"tex\">F</span> and to <span class=\"tex\">\\ell</span> are the same. Because <span class=\"tex\">V</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span> are on the parabola, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">p=F V=V C</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 p=F C=B D=F B</span>. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <span class=\"tex\">V B=\\sqrt{F V^{2}+F B^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} p</span>, and thus <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (\\angle F V B)=\\dfrac{F V}{V B}=\\dfrac{p}{\\sqrt{5} p}=</span> <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A V B=2(\\angle F V B)</span>, it follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\cos (\\angle A V B)=2 \\cos ^{2}(\\angle F V B)-1=2\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)^{2}-1=\\dfrac{2}{5}-1=-\\dfrac{3}{5}</span></p><center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-217.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>Establish as in the first solution that <span class=\"tex\">F V=p, F B=2 p</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">V B=\\sqrt{5} p</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">A B=2 \\cdot F B=4 p</span>, and by the Law of Cosines applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B V</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\cos \\angle A V B=\\dfrac{V A^{2}+V B^{2}-A B^{2}}{2(V A)(V B)}=\\dfrac{5 p^{2}+5 p^{2}-16 p^{2}}{2\\left(5 p^{2}\\right)}= \\boxed{-\\dfrac{3}{5}}</span></p><p>Note: The segment <span class=\"tex\">A B</span> is called the latus rectum.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> How many positive two-digit integers are factors of <span class=\"tex\">2^{24}-1?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">4</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">8</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">10</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">12</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">14</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Factoring results in the following product of primes:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n2^{24}-1 =\\left(2^{12}-1\\right)\\left(2^{12}+1\\right)=\\left(2^{6}-1\\right)\\left(2^{6}+1\\right)\\left(2^{4}+1\\right)\\left(2^{8}-2^{4}+1\\right) \\\\\n=63 \\cdot 65 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 241=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 241\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>The two-digit integers that can be formed from these prime factors are:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{r}\n17, \\quad 3 \\cdot 17=51, \\quad 5 \\cdot 17=85 \\\\\n13, \\quad 3 \\cdot 13=39, \\quad 5 \\cdot 13=65, \\quad 7 \\cdot 13=91 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 7=21, \\quad 5 \\cdot 7=35, \\quad 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7=63 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 5=15, \\quad \\text { and } \\quad 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5=45\n\\end{array}</span></p><p>Thus there are <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{12}</span> positive two-digit factors.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> Rhombus <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> has side length <span class=\"tex\">2</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B=120^{\\circ}</span>. Region <span class=\"tex\">R</span> consists of all points inside the rhombus that are closer to vertex <span class=\"tex\">B</span> than any of the other three vertices. What is the area of <span class=\"tex\">R?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">1+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">2</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> and <span class=\"tex\">H</span> be the midpoints of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B C}</span>, respectively. The line drawn perpendicular to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> through <span class=\"tex\">E</span> divides the rhombus into two regions: points that are closer to vertex <span class=\"tex\">A</span> than <span class=\"tex\">B</span>, and points that are closer to vertex <span class=\"tex\">B</span> than <span class=\"tex\">A</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">F</span> be the intersection of this line with diagonal <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span>. Similarly, let point <span class=\"tex\">G</span> be the intersection of the diagonal <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> with the perpendicular to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B C}</span> drawn from <span class=\"tex\">H</span>. Then the desired region <span class=\"tex\">R</span> is the pentagon <span class=\"tex\">B E F G H</span>.</p>\n<p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A F E</span> is a <span class=\"tex\">30-60-90^{\\circ}</span> triangle with <span class=\"tex\">A E=1</span>. Hence the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A F E</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}</span>. Both <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B F E</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B G H</span> are congruent to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A F E</span>, so they have the same areas. Also <span class=\"tex\">\\angle F B G=120^{\\circ}-\\angle F B E-\\angle G B H=</span>\\<br/>\n<span class=\"tex\">60^{\\circ}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle F B G</span> is an equilateral triangle. In fact, the altitude from <span class=\"tex\">B</span> to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{F G}</span> divides <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle F B G</span> into two triangles, each congruent to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A F E</span>. Hence the area of <span class=\"tex\">B E F G H</span> is <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-218.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=10^{10 x}, g(x)=\\log _{10}\\left(\\dfrac{x}{10}\\right), h_{1}(x)=g(f(x))</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">h_{n}(x)=h_{1}\\left(h_{n-1}(x)\\right)</span> for integers <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 2</span>. What is the sum of the digits of <span class=\"tex\">h_{2011}(1)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">16,081</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16,089</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18,089</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18,098</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18,099</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">16,081</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">16,089</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">18,089</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">18,098</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">18,099</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">h_{1}(x)=\\log _{10}\\left(\\dfrac{10^{10 x}}{10}\\right)=\\log _{10}\\left(10^{10 x-1}\\right)=10 x-1</span></p><p>Therefore <span class=\"tex\">h_{2}(x)=10^{2} x-(1+10), h_{3}(x)=10^{3} x-\\left(1+10+10^{2}\\right)</span>, and in general,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">h_{n}(x)=10^{n} x-\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} 10^{k}</span></p><p>Hence <span class=\"tex\">h_{n}(1)</span> is an <span class=\"tex\">n</span>-digit integer whose units digit is 9 and whose other digits are all 8 's. The sum of the digits of <span class=\"tex\">h_{2011}(1)</span> is <span class=\"tex\">8 \\cdot 2010+9= \\boxed{16,089}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> A pyramid has a square base with sides of length <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and has lateral faces that are equilateral triangles. A cube is placed within the pyramid so that one face is on the base of the pyramid and its opposite face has all its edges on the lateral faces of the pyramid. What is the volume of this cube?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5 \\sqrt{2}-7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7-4 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{27}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">5 \\sqrt{2}-7</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">7-4 \\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{27}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">A</span> be the apex of the pyramid, and let the base be the square <span class=\"tex\">B C D E</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">A B=A D=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B D=\\sqrt{2}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B A D</span> is an isosceles right triangle. Let the cube have edge length <span class=\"tex\">x</span>. The intersection of the cube with the plane of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B A D</span> is a rectangle with height <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and width <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2} x</span>. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}=B D=2 x+\\sqrt{2} x</span>, from which <span class=\"tex\">x=\\sqrt{2}-1</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-218_1_.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>Hence the cube has volume</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(\\sqrt{2}-1)^{3}=(\\sqrt{2})^{3}-3(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+3 \\sqrt{2}-1= \\boxed{5 \\sqrt{2}-7}</span></p><p>OR</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">A</span> be the apex of the pyramid, let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> be the center of the base, let <span class=\"tex\">P</span> be the midpoint of one base edge, and let the cube intersect <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A P}</span> at <span class=\"tex\">Q</span>. Let a coordinate plane intersect the pyramid so that <span class=\"tex\">O</span> is the origin, <span class=\"tex\">A</span> on the positive <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis, and <span class=\"tex\">P=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, 0\\right)</span>. Segment <span class=\"tex\">A P</span> is an altitude of a lateral side of the pyramid, so <span class=\"tex\">A P=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}</span>, and it follows that <span class=\"tex\">A=\\left(0, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)</span>. Thus the equation of line <span class=\"tex\">A P</span> is <span class=\"tex\">y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\sqrt{2} x</span>. If the side length of the cube is <span class=\"tex\">s</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">Q=\\left(\\dfrac{s}{2}, s\\right)</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">s=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{2}</span>. Solving gives <span class=\"tex\">s=\\sqrt{2}-1</span>, and the result follows that in the first solution.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> A lattice point in an <span class=\"tex\">x y</span>-coordinate system is any point <span class=\"tex\">(x, y)</span> where both <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> are integers. The graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=m x+2</span> passes through no lattice point with <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;x \\leq 100</span> for all <span class=\"tex\">m</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}&lt;m&lt;a</span>. What is the maximum possible value of <span class=\"tex\">a?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{101}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{50}{99}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{100}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{52}{101}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{13}{25}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{101}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{50}{99}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{100}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{52}{101}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{13}{25}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;x \\leq 100</span>, the nearest lattice point directly above the line <span class=\"tex\">y=\\dfrac{1}{2} x+2</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\left(x, \\dfrac{1}{2} x+3\\right)</span> if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is even and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(x, \\dfrac{1}{2} x+\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)</span> if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is odd. The slope of the line that contains this point and <span class=\"tex\">(0,2)</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{x}</span> if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is even and <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2 x}</span> if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is odd. The minimum value of the slope is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{51}{100}</span> if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is even and <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{50}{99}</span> if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is odd. Therefore the line <span class=\"tex\">y=m x+2</span> contains no lattice point with <span class=\"tex\">0&lt;x \\leq 100</span> for <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}&lt;m&lt; \\boxed{\\dfrac{50}{99}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Triangle <span class=\"tex\">A B C</span> has <span class=\"tex\">A B=13, B C=14</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">A C=15</span>. The points <span class=\"tex\">D, E</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">F</span> are the midpoints of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}, \\overline{B C}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> respectively. Let <span class=\"tex\">X \\neq E</span> be the intersection of the circumcircles of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B D E</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C E F</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">X A+X B+</span> <span class=\"tex\">X C ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">24</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{195}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{129 \\sqrt{7}}{14}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{69 \\sqrt{2}}{4}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">24</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">14 \\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{195}{8}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{129 \\sqrt{7}}{14}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{69 \\sqrt{2}}{4}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{D E}</span> is parallel to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{E F}</span> is parallel to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B D E=\\angle B A C=\\angle E F C</span>. By the Inscribed Angle Theorem, <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B D E=\\angle B X E</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle E F C=\\angle E X C</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B X E=\\angle E X C</span>. Furthermore <span class=\"tex\">B E=E C</span>, so by the Angle Bisector Theorem <span class=\"tex\">X B=X C</span>. Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B X C=2 \\angle B X E=2 \\angle B D E=2 \\angle B A C</span>, and by the Inscribed Angle Theorem, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">X</span> is the circumcenter of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">X A=X B=X C=R</span> the circumradius of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-219.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">a=B C, b=A C</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c=A B</span>. The area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> equals <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4 R}(a b c)</span>, and by Heron's Formula it also equals <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">s=\\dfrac{1}{2}(a+b+c)</span>. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">R=\\dfrac{a b c}{4 \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}}=\\dfrac{13 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 15}{4 \\sqrt{21 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6}}=\\dfrac{65}{8}</span></p><p>and <span class=\"tex\">X A+X B+X C=3 R= \\boxed{\\dfrac{195}{8}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> The arithmetic mean of two distinct positive integers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> is a two-digit integer. The geometric mean of <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> is obtained by reversing the digits of the arithmetic mean. What is <span class=\"tex\">|x-y|?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">24</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">48</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">54</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">66</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">70</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">24</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">48</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">54</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">66</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">70</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let the arithmetic and geometric means of <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> be <span class=\"tex\">10 a+b</span> and <span class=\"tex\">10 b+a</span>, respectively. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{x+y}{2}=10 a+b \\Rightarrow(x+y)^{2}=400 a^{2}+80 a b+4 b^{2}</span></p><p>and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sqrt{x y}=10 b+a \\Rightarrow x y=100 b^{2}+20 a b+a^{2}</span></p><p>So</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(x-y)^{2}=(x+y)^{2}-4 x y=396\\left(a^{2}-b^{2}\\right)=11 \\cdot 6^{2} \\cdot(a+b)(a-b)</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> are distinct, <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are distinct digits, and the last expression is a perfect square if and only if <span class=\"tex\">a+b=11</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a-b</span> is a perfect square. The cases <span class=\"tex\">a-b=1,4</span>, and 9 give solutions <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)=(6,5),(7.5,3.5)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(10,1)</span>, respectively. Because <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are digits only the first solution is valid. Thus <span class=\"tex\">(x-y)^{2}=11 \\cdot 6^{2} \\cdot 11=66^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">|x-y|=\\boxed{66}</span>. Note that the given conditions are satisfied if <span class=\"tex\">\\{x, y\\}=\\{32,98\\}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">T_{1}</span> be a triangle with sides <span class=\"tex\">2011,2012</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">2013</span>. For <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 1</span>, if <span class=\"tex\">T_{n}=\\triangle A B C</span> and <span class=\"tex\">D, E</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">F</span> are the points of tangency of the incircle of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> to the sides <span class=\"tex\">A B, B C</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">A C</span>, respectively, then <span class=\"tex\">T_{n+1}</span> is a triangle with side lengths <span class=\"tex\">A D, B E</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C F</span>, if it exists. What is the perimeter of the last triangle in the sequence <span class=\"tex\">\\left(T_{n}\\right)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{32}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{64}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{128}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{256}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{8}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{32}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{64}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{128}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{256}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">T_{n}=\\triangle A B C</span>. Suppose <span class=\"tex\">a=B C, b=A C</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c=A B</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B E}</span> are both tangent to the incircle of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">B D=B E</span>. Similarly, <span class=\"tex\">A D=A F</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C E=C F</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n2 B E =B E+B D=B E+C E+B D+A D-(A F+C F) \\\\\n=a+c-b\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>that is, <span class=\"tex\">B E=\\dfrac{1}{2}(a+c-b)</span>. Similarly <span class=\"tex\">A D=\\dfrac{1}{2}(b+c-a)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C F=\\dfrac{1}{2}(a+b-c)</span>. In the given <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span>, suppose that <span class=\"tex\">A B=x+1, B C=x-1</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">A C=x</span>. Using the formulas for <span class=\"tex\">B E, A D</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C F</span> derived before, it must be true that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nB E =\\dfrac{1}{2}((x-1)+(x+1)-x)=\\dfrac{1}{2} x \\\\\nA D =\\dfrac{1}{2}(x+(x+1)-(x-1))=\\dfrac{1}{2} x+1, \\text { and } \\\\\nC F =\\dfrac{1}{2}((x-1)+x-(x+1))=\\dfrac{1}{2} x-1\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Hence both <span class=\"tex\">(B C, C A, A B)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(C F, B E, A D)</span> are of the form <span class=\"tex\">(y-1, y, y+1)</span>. This is independent of the values of <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, so it holds for all <span class=\"tex\">T_{n}</span>. Furthermore, adding the formulas for <span class=\"tex\">B E, A D</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">C F</span> shows that the perimeter of\\<br/>\n<span class=\"tex\">T_{n+1}</span> equals <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}(a+b+c)</span>, and consequently the perimeter of the last triangle <span class=\"tex\">T_{N}</span> in the sequence is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{2^{N-1}}(2011+2012+2013)=\\dfrac{1509}{2^{N-3}}</span></p><p>The last member <span class=\"tex\">T_{N}</span> of the sequence will fail to define a successor if for the first time the new lengths fail the Triangle Inequality, that is, if</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">-1+\\dfrac{2012}{2^{N}}+\\dfrac{2012}{2^{N}} \\leq 1+\\dfrac{2012}{2^{N}}</span></p><p>Equivalently, <span class=\"tex\">2012 \\leq 2^{N+1}</span> which happens for the first time when <span class=\"tex\">N=10</span>. Thus the required perimeter of <span class=\"tex\">T_{N}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1509}{2^{7}}= \\boxed{\\dfrac{1509}{128}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> A bug travels in the coordinate plane, moving only along the lines that are parallel to the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis or <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis. Let <span class=\"tex\">A=(-3,2)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B=(3,-2)</span>. Consider all possible paths of the bug from <span class=\"tex\">A</span> to <span class=\"tex\">B</span> of length at most <span class=\"tex\">20</span>. How many points with integer coordinates lie on at least one of these paths?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">161</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">185</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">195</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">227</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">255</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">161</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">185</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">195</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">227</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">255</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">X=(x, y)</span>. The distance traveled by the bug from <span class=\"tex\">A</span> to <span class=\"tex\">X</span> is at least <span class=\"tex\">|x+3|+|y-2|</span>. Similarly, the distance traveled by the bug from <span class=\"tex\">X</span> to <span class=\"tex\">B</span> is at least <span class=\"tex\">|x-3|+|y+2|</span>. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">X</span> belongs to a path from <span class=\"tex\">A</span> to <span class=\"tex\">B</span> traveled by the bug if and only if</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">d=|x-3|+|x+3|+|y-2|+|y+2| \\leq 20</span></p><p>The expression for <span class=\"tex\">d</span> is invariant if <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is replaced by <span class=\"tex\">-x</span> or <span class=\"tex\">y</span> is replaced by <span class=\"tex\">-y</span>. By symmetry, it is enough to count the number of points <span class=\"tex\">X</span> with <span class=\"tex\">x \\geq 0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y \\geq 0</span>, multiply by 4 , and subtract the points that were overcounted, that is those in the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis or in the <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis. Consider four cases:</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-221.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>\n<p>Case 1. <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq x \\leq 3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq y \\leq 2</span>. In this case <span class=\"tex\">|x-3|+|x+3|=6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">|y-2|+|y+2|=4</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">d=10&lt;20</span> and there are <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot 3=12</span> points <span class=\"tex\">X</span> in this case. This includes the origin and 5 other points for which <span class=\"tex\">x y=0</span>.</p>\n<p>Case 2. <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq x \\leq 3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y \\geq 3</span>. In this case <span class=\"tex\">|x-3|+|x+3|=6</span> and <span class=\"tex\">|y-2|+|y+2|=</span> <span class=\"tex\">2 y</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">d=6+2 y \\leq 20</span> if and only if <span class=\"tex\">y \\leq 7</span>. There are <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot 5=20</span> points <span class=\"tex\">X</span> in this case. This includes 5 points for which <span class=\"tex\">x y=0</span>.</p>\n<p>Case 3. <span class=\"tex\">x \\geq 4</span> and <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq y \\leq 2</span>. In this case <span class=\"tex\">|x-3|+|x+3|=2 x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">|y-2|+|y+2|=4</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">d=4+2 x \\leq 20</span> if and only if <span class=\"tex\">x \\leq 8</span>. There are <span class=\"tex\">5 \\cdot 3=15</span> points <span class=\"tex\">X</span> in this case. This includes 5 points for which <span class=\"tex\">x y=0</span>.</p>\n<p>Case 4. <span class=\"tex\">x \\geq 4</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y \\geq 3</span>. In this case <span class=\"tex\">|x-3|+|x+3|=2 x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">|y-2|+|y+2|=2 y</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">d=2 x+2 y \\leq 20</span> if and only if <span class=\"tex\">x+y \\leq 10</span>. The number of points <span class=\"tex\">X</span> in this case is equal to</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{x=4}^{7} \\sum_{y=3}^{10-x} 1=\\sum_{x=4}^{7}(10-x-2)=\\sum_{x=4}^{7}(8-x)=4+3+2+1=10</span></p><p>and there are no points with <span class=\"tex\">x y=0</span>.</p>\n<p>By symmetry the required total is <span class=\"tex\">4(12+20+15+10)-2(5+5+5)-3=</span> <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot 57-2 \\cdot 15-3= \\boxed{195}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">P(z)=z^{8}+(4 \\sqrt{3}+6) z^{4}-(4 \\sqrt{3}+7)</span>. What is the minimum perimeter among all the <span class=\"tex\">8</span>-sided polygons in the complex plane whose vertices are precisely the zeros of <span class=\"tex\">P(z)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{3}+4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{2}+3 \\sqrt{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{2}+4 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{3}+6</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{3}+4</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">8 \\sqrt{2}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{2}+3 \\sqrt{6}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{2}+4 \\sqrt{3}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{3}+6</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Factoring or using the quadratic formula with <span class=\"tex\">z^{4}</span> as the variable yields <span class=\"tex\">P(z)=\\left(z^{4}-1\\right)\\left(z^{4}+(4 \\sqrt{3}+7)\\right)</span>. Moreover, <span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{3}+7=(\\sqrt{3}+2)^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2(\\sqrt{3}+2)=2 \\sqrt{3}+4=(\\sqrt{3}+1)^{2}</span>; thus <span class=\"tex\">4 \\sqrt{3}+7=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2})\\right)^{4}</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">w=\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}+</span> 1 ), then the eight zeros of <span class=\"tex\">P(z)</span> are <span class=\"tex\">1,-1, i,-i, w(1+i), w(-1+i), w(-1-i)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">w(1-i)</span>.</p>\n<p>The distances from 1 to the other zeros are</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{r}\n|1-(-1)|=2,|1 \\pm i|=\\sqrt{2},|1-w(1 \\pm i)|=\\sqrt{(1-w)^{2}+w^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}, \\text { and } \\\\\n|1-w(-1 \\pm i)|=\\sqrt{(1+w)^{2}+w^{2}}=\\sqrt{2 \\sqrt{3}+4}=\\sqrt{3}+1\n\\end{array}</span></p><p>Similarly, the distances from <span class=\"tex\">w(1+i)</span> to the other zeros are</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{r}\n|w(1+i)-w(1-i)|=|w(1+i)-w(-1+i)|=2 w=\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\\n|w(1+i)-w(-1-i)|=2 \\sqrt{2} w=\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{array}</span></p><p>and by symmetry,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{r}\n|w(1+i)-1|=|w(1+i)-i|=\\sqrt{2}, \\text { and } \\\\\n|w(1+i)+1|=|w(1+i)+i|=\\sqrt{3}+1\n\\end{array}</span></p><p>Because the set of zeros is 4 -fold symmetric with respect to the origin, it follows that every line segment joining two of the zeros has length at least <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{2}</span>. This shows that any polygon with vertices at the zeros has perimeter at least <span class=\"tex\">8 \\sqrt{2}</span>. Finally, note that the polygon with consecutive vertices <span class=\"tex\">1, w(1+i), i, w(-1+i)</span>, <span class=\"tex\">-1, w(-1-i),-i</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">w(1-i)</span> has perimeter <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{8 \\sqrt{2}}</span>.</p>\n<center>\n<div><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2011B/2024_06_22_1fefda5e99366fb06e42g-223.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n</center>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> For every <span class=\"tex\">m</span> and <span class=\"tex\">k</span> integers with <span class=\"tex\">k</span> odd, denote by <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{m}{k}\\right]</span> the integer closest to <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{m}{k}</span>. For every odd integer <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, let <span class=\"tex\">P(k)</span> be the probability that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left[\\dfrac{n}{k}\\right]+\\left[\\dfrac{100-n}{k}\\right]=\\left[\\dfrac{100}{k}\\right]</span></p><p>for an integer <span class=\"tex\">n</span> randomly chosen from the interval <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq n \\leq 99!</span>. What is the minimum possible value of <span class=\"tex\">P(k)</span> over the odd integers <span class=\"tex\">k</span> in the interval <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq 99 ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{50}{99}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{44}{87}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{34}{67}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{13}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>A. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span><br/>\nB. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{50}{99}</span><br/>\nC. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{44}{87}</span><br/>\nD. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{34}{67}</span><br/>\nE. <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{7}{13}</span><br/>\n</p>\n<p>Let</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n100 =q k+r, \\text { with } q, r \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\text { and }|r| \\leq \\dfrac{k-1}{2}, \\text { and } \\\\\nn =q_{1} k+r_{1}, \\text { with } q_{1}, r_{1} \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\text { and }\\left|r_{1}\\right| \\leq \\dfrac{k-1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>so that <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{100}{k}\\right]=q</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{n}{k}\\right]=q_{1}</span>. Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{n+m k}{k}\\right]=\\left[\\dfrac{n}{k}\\right]+m</span> for every integer <span class=\"tex\">m</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">n</span> satisfies the required identity if and only if <span class=\"tex\">n+m k</span> satisfies the identity for all integers <span class=\"tex\">m</span>. Thus all members of a residue class <span class=\"tex\">\\bmod k</span> either satisfy the required equality or not; moreover, <span class=\"tex\">k</span> divides 99 ! for every <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq 99</span>, so every residue class mod <span class=\"tex\">k</span> in the interval <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq n \\leq 99</span> ! has the same number of elements. Suppose <span class=\"tex\">r \\geq 0</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">r_{1} \\geq r-\\dfrac{k-1}{2}</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">100-n=\\left(q-q_{1}\\right) k+\\left(r-r_{1}\\right)</span></p><p>where <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq r-r_{1} \\leq \\dfrac{k-1}{2}</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{100-n}{k}\\right]=q-q_{1}=\\left[\\dfrac{100}{k}\\right]-\\left[\\dfrac{n}{k}\\right]</span>. Similarly, if <span class=\"tex\">r_{1}&lt;r-\\dfrac{\\bar{k}-1}{2}</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">100-n=\\left(q-q_{1}+1\\right) k+\\left(r-r_{1}-k\\right)</span></p><p>where <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{k-1}{2} \\leq r-r_{1}-k \\leq-1</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{100-n}{k}\\right]=q-q_{1}+1&gt;\\left[\\dfrac{100}{k}\\right]-\\left[\\dfrac{n}{k}\\right]</span>. It follows that the only residue classes <span class=\"tex\">r_{1}</span> that satisfy the identity are those in the interval <span class=\"tex\">r-\\dfrac{k-1}{2} \\leq r_{1} \\leq \\dfrac{k-1}{2}</span>. Thus for <span class=\"tex\">r \\geq 0</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(k)=\\dfrac{1}{k}\\left(\\dfrac{k-1}{2}+1-\\left(r-\\dfrac{k-1}{2}\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac{k-r}{k}=1-\\dfrac{|r|}{k}</span></p><p>Similarly, if <span class=\"tex\">r&lt;0</span> then the identity is satisfied only by the residue classes <span class=\"tex\">r_{1}</span> in the interval <span class=\"tex\">-\\dfrac{k-1}{2} \\leq r_{1} \\leq r+\\dfrac{k-1}{2}</span>. Thus for <span class=\"tex\">r&lt;0</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(k)=\\dfrac{1}{k}\\left(r+\\dfrac{k-1}{2}+1-\\left(-\\dfrac{k-1}{2}\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac{k+r}{k}=1-\\dfrac{|r|}{k}</span></p><p>To minimize <span class=\"tex\">P(k)</span> in the range <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq 99</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">k</span> is odd, first suppose that <span class=\"tex\">r=\\dfrac{k-1}{2}</span>. Note that <span class=\"tex\">P(k)=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2 k}, 100=q k+\\dfrac{k-1}{2}</span>, and so <span class=\"tex\">201=k(2 q+1)</span>.</p>\n<p>The minimum of <span class=\"tex\">P(k)</span> in this case is achieved by the largest possible <span class=\"tex\">k</span> under this restriction. Because <span class=\"tex\">201=3 \\cdot 67</span>, it follows that the largest factor <span class=\"tex\">k</span> of 201 in the given range is <span class=\"tex\">k=67</span>. In this case <span class=\"tex\">P(67)=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2 \\cdot 67}=\\dfrac{34}{67}</span>. Second, suppose <span class=\"tex\">r=\\dfrac{1-k}{2}</span>. In this case <span class=\"tex\">P(k)=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2 k}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">199=k(2 q-1)</span>. Because 199 is prime, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">k=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">P(k)=1&gt;\\dfrac{34}{67}</span>. Finally, if <span class=\"tex\">|r| \\leq \\dfrac{k-3}{2}</span>, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(k) =1-\\dfrac{|r|}{k}&gt;1-\\dfrac{k-3}{2 k}=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{3}{2 k} \\\\\n\\geq \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{3}{2 \\cdot 99}&gt;\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2 \\cdot 67}=\\dfrac{34}{67}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Therefore the minimum value of <span class=\"tex\">P(k)</span> in the required range is <span class=\"tex\">\\boxed{\\dfrac{34}{67}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2011/part-b/problem-25"}]});
